Anthracycline-Induced Cardiotoxicity
Haematology & Oncology
Exertional dyspnoea/orthopnoea, Bilateral peripheral pitting oedema, History of doxorubicin exposure, New-onset decline in LVEF

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-004
Reason for Visit: Acute-onset orthopnoea and worsening peripheral oedema requiring urgent evaluation to prevent acute decompensated heart failure and respiratory distress overnight.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a breast cancer survivor experiencing sudden awakenings gasping for air and an inability to lie flat. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for biventricular failure and volume overload, as waiting until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of acute pulmonary oedema and respiratory failure.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't catch my breath when I lie down, I have to sleep on three pillows, and my legs have swollen up like balloons over the last few days."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of progressive exertional dyspnoea over the last 3 weeks, culminating in acute orthopnoea over the past 48 hours. Last night, they experienced paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, waking up suddenly gasping for air. They note new, heavy swelling in both lower legs. The patient completed a chemotherapy regimen for breast cancer including doxorubicin 12 months ago. They complain of generalized severe fatigue and profound weakness.
Functional Status: Mobility heavily restricted by dyspnoea; unable to perform domestic chores.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to access upstairs bedroom due to breathlessness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 82 Kg (up 4 kg in one week)
General: Fatigued, speaking in short sentences. Mild respiratory distress when supine, preferring to sit upright.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Auscultation reveals a distinct third heart sound (S3 gallop). Jugular venous pressure (JVP) is elevated at 4 cm above the sternal angle.
Respiratory: Fine bibasal crepitations heard on auscultation, not clearing with coughing.
Extremities: 2+ bilateral peripheral pitting oedema extending to the mid-calves. Calves are soft, non-tender.
Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, slight hepatojugular reflux present.
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a recliner chair in the lounge; multiple pillows stacked.
Medication Reconciliation: No previous cardiac medications. Recent oncology review noted, but no recent echocardiogram on file.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anthracycline-Induced Cardiotoxicity (Decompensated Heart Failure) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) with Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: bilateral nature of the oedema, S3 gallop, and orthopnoea pointing toward pump failure rather than isolated right heart strain.

Community-Acquired Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of fever, purulent sputum, and presence of classic volume overload signs (elevated JVP, S3).

Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of wheeze, clear history of orthopnoea, and presence of pitting oedema and S3 gallop.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's presentation of orthopnoea, paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, and bibasal crackles strongly indicates acute decompensated heart failure. The history of doxorubicin exposure raises immediate concern for anthracycline-induced cardiotoxicity with a likely newly documented decline in LVEF. The presence of an S3 gallop and resting hypoxia (SpO2 92%) signifies precarious haemodynamic and respiratory status. Delaying this assessment until regular hours would risk fatal acute pulmonary oedema overnight. The urgent home visit was critically necessary to diagnose the volume overload and facilitate immediate medical offloading and escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate administration of oral furosemide 40mg stat to initiate diuresis. Urgent transfer to the Emergency Department for intravenous diuretics, continuous monitoring, and a formal transthoracic echocardiogram to assess LVEF.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Continue sitting strictly upright with legs dangling if possible. By using gravity to keep fluid pooled in the lower extremities, you decrease the immediate fluid return to the heart, easing breathing.

Restrict all oral fluid intake tonight. By limiting new fluid entering your system, you prevent worsening of the volume overload in your lungs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required. Call 000 if breathing suddenly worsens, if you develop chest pain, or if you become confused or dizzy.

